1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Are we ready?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:11,990
Okay, ready for me to start.

3
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:23,980
Ready for the taping to start in a minute.

4
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:38,630
He's going to raise his hand and signal when I'm on.

5
00:00:38,630 --> 00:00:50,620
Just a minute, though, let them settle.0 Okay, guys.

6
00:00:50,620 --> 00:01:02,410
Okay, give me the signal, then, when you want me to

7
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:02,910
start.

8
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:07,280
Okay, this is linear algebra, lecture four.

9
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,540
And, the first thing I have to do

10
00:01:11,540 --> 00:01:14,920
is something that was on the list for last time,

11
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,320
but here it is now.

12
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:19,190
What's the inverse of a product?

13
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:22,070
If I multiply two matrices together

14
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:25,900
and I know their inverses, how do I

15
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:29,250
get the inverse of A times B?

16
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:34,520
So I know what inverses mean for a single matrix A

17
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:37,360
and for a matrix B.

18
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:44,200
What matrix do I multiply by to get the identity if I have A B?

19
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:47,770
Okay, that'll be simple but so basic.

20
00:01:47,770 --> 00:01:52,240
Then I'm going to use that to --

21
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:56,650
I will have a product of matrices and the product

22
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:59,620
that we'll meet will be these elimination matrices

23
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:02,440
and the net result of today's lectures

24
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:07,170
is the big formula for elimination,

25
00:02:07,170 --> 00:02:08,889
so the net result of today's lecture

26
00:02:08,889 --> 00:02:18,350
is this great way to look at Gaussian elimination.

27
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:22,050
We know that we get from A to U by elimination.

28
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:26,220
We know the steps -- but now we get the right way to look

29
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:28,930
at it, A equals L U.

30
00:02:28,930 --> 00:02:32,051
So that's the high point for today.

31
00:02:32,051 --> 00:02:32,550
Okay.

32
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:37,070
Can I take the easy part, the first step first?

33
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:42,940
So, suppose A is invertible -- and of course it's going to be

34
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:46,550
a big question, when is the matrix invertible?

35
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:51,160
But let's say A is invertible and B is invertible,

36
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:55,620
then what matrix gives me the inverse of A B?

37
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:58,080
So that's the direct question.

38
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:59,990
What's the inverse of A B?

39
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:03,150
Do I multiply those separate inverses?

40
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:04,370
Yes.

41
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:08,610
I multiply the two matrices A inverse and B inverse,

42
00:03:08,610 --> 00:03:11,700
but what order do I multiply?

43
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,340
In reverse order.

44
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:14,810
And you see why.

45
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:20,490
So the right thing to put here is B inverse A inverse.

46
00:03:20,490 --> 00:03:22,960
That's the inverse I'm after.

47
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:30,070
We can just check that A B times that matrix gives the identity.

48
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:31,260
Okay.

49
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:35,110
So why -- once again, it's this fact that I can move

50
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:37,330
parentheses around.

51
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:42,200
I can just erase them all and do the multiplications any way

52
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,190
I want to.

53
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:47,040
So what's the right multiplication to do first?

54
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:48,160
B times B inverse.

55
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,310
This product here I is the identity.

56
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:55,410
Then A times the identity is the identity

57
00:03:55,410 --> 00:04:01,000
and then finally A times A inverse gives the identity.

58
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,470
So forgive the dumb example in the book.

59
00:04:06,470 --> 00:04:12,070
Why do you, do the inverse things in reverse order?

60
00:04:12,070 --> 00:04:14,920
It's just like -- you take off your shoes,

61
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:19,000
you take off your socks, then the good way to invert that

62
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,140
process is socks back on first, then shoes.

63
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:27,460
Sorry, okay.

64
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:31,330
I'm sorry that's on the tape.

65
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:35,740
And, of course, on the other side we should really just

66
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:37,960
check -- on the other side I have B inverse,

67
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:39,290
A inverse.

68
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:43,110
That does multiply A B, and this time it's

69
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:45,880
these guys that give the identity,

70
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:48,790
squeeze down, they give the identity, we're in shape.

71
00:04:48,790 --> 00:04:49,380
Okay.

72
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:52,960
So there's the inverse.

73
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:53,600
Good.

74
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:56,710
While we're at it, let me do a transpose,

75
00:04:56,710 --> 00:04:59,750
because the next lecture has got a lot to --

76
00:04:59,750 --> 00:05:00,960
involves transposes.

77
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:05,290
So how do I -- if I transpose a matrix,

78
00:05:05,290 --> 00:05:09,040
I'm talking about square, invertible matrices right now.

79
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:14,550
If I transpose one, what's its inverse?

80
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:18,210
Well, the nice formula is --

81
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:18,710
let's see.

82
00:05:18,710 --> 00:05:23,030
Let me start from A, A inverse equal the identity.

83
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:25,830
So -- give me a first row.

84
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:30,590
And let me transpose both sides.

85
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:34,260
That will bring a transpose into the picture.

86
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:39,490
So if I transpose the identity matrix, what do I have?

87
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:40,800
The identity, right?

88
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,570
If I exchange rows and columns, the identity

89
00:05:44,570 --> 00:05:45,990
is a symmetric matrix.

90
00:05:45,990 --> 00:05:48,000
It doesn't know the difference.

91
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,670
If I transpose these guys, that product, then again

92
00:05:54,670 --> 00:06:00,290
it turns out that I have to reverse the order.

93
00:06:00,290 --> 00:06:04,120
I can transpose them separately, but when I multiply,

94
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:07,040
those transposes come in the opposite order.

95
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:12,160
So it's A inverse transpose times A transpose

96
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:13,340
giving the identity.

97
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:17,971
So that's -- this equation is -- just comes directly from that

98
00:06:17,971 --> 00:06:18,470
one.

99
00:06:18,470 --> 00:06:21,710
But this equation tells me what I wanted to know,

100
00:06:21,710 --> 00:06:26,720
namely what is the inverse of this guy A transpose?

101
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:31,170
What's the inverse of that -- if I transpose a matrix,

102
00:06:31,170 --> 00:06:34,370
what'ss the inverse of the result?

103
00:06:34,370 --> 00:06:39,170
And this equation tells me that here it is.

104
00:06:39,170 --> 00:06:45,900
This is the inverse of A transpose.

105
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:49,740
Inverse of A transpose.

106
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:52,620
Of A transpose.

107
00:06:52,620 --> 00:07:00,310
So I'll put a big circle around that,

108
00:07:00,310 --> 00:07:04,620
because that's the answer, that's the best

109
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:07,700
answer we could hope for.

110
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:13,040
That if you want to know the inverse of A transpose

111
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:17,940
and you know the inverse of A, then you just transpose that.

112
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,260
So in a -- to put it another way,

113
00:07:20,260 --> 00:07:24,370
transposing and inversing you can do in either order

114
00:07:24,370 --> 00:07:26,610
for a single matrix.

115
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:27,540
Okay.

116
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:31,950
So these are like basic facts that we can now use,

117
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:36,190
all right -- so now I put it to use.

118
00:07:36,190 --> 00:07:40,440
I put it to use by thinking -- we're really completing,

119
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:44,380
the subject of elimination.

120
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:52,070
Actually, -- the thing about elimination is it's the right

121
00:07:52,070 --> 00:07:53,820
way to understand what the matrix has got.

122
00:07:53,820 --> 00:07:59,110
This A equal L U is the most basic factorization

123
00:07:59,110 --> 00:08:04,370
of a matrix.

124
00:08:04,370 --> 00:08:09,460
I always worry that you will think

125
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:15,210
this course is all elimination.

126
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:18,540
It's just row operations.

127
00:08:18,540 --> 00:08:21,360
And please don't.

128
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:32,669
We'll be beyond that, but it's the right algebra to do first.

129
00:08:32,669 --> 00:08:33,370
Okay.

130
00:08:33,370 --> 00:08:37,320
So, now I'm coming near the end of it,

131
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:40,150
but I want to get it in a decent form.

132
00:08:40,150 --> 00:08:43,809
So my decent form is matrix form.

133
00:08:43,809 --> 00:08:48,530
I have a matrix A, let's suppose it's a good matrix,

134
00:08:48,530 --> 00:08:51,030
I can do elimination, no row exchanges --

135
00:08:51,030 --> 00:08:54,540
So no row exchanges for now.

136
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:59,710
Pivots all fine, nothing zero in the pivot position.

137
00:08:59,710 --> 00:09:03,350
I get to the very end, which is U.

138
00:09:03,350 --> 00:09:06,180
So I get from A to U.

139
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:09,410
And I want to know what's the connection?

140
00:09:09,410 --> 00:09:10,640
STUDENT: Zero one zero?

141
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:13,060
How is A related to U?

142
00:09:13,060 --> 00:09:16,700
And this is going to tell me that there's

143
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,031
a matrix L that connects them.

144
00:09:19,031 --> 00:09:19,530
Okay.

145
00:09:19,530 --> 00:09:23,480
Can I do it for a two by two first?

146
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:23,980
Okay.

147
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:25,600
Two by two, elimination.

148
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:28,340
Okay, so I'll do it under here.

149
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:28,840
Okay.

150
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:34,470
So let my matrix A be --

151
00:09:34,470 --> 00:09:37,850
We'll keep it simple, say two and an eight,

152
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:41,150
so we know that the first pivot is a two,

153
00:09:41,150 --> 00:09:44,060
and the multiplier's going to be a four

154
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:47,190
and then let me put a one here and what number

155
00:09:47,190 --> 00:09:50,460
do I not want to put there?

156
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:51,330
Four.

157
00:09:51,330 --> 00:09:54,420
I don't want a four there, because in that case,

158
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:58,930
the second pivot would not -- we wouldn't have a second pivot.

159
00:09:58,930 --> 00:10:02,580
The matrix would be singular, general screw-up.

160
00:10:02,580 --> 00:10:03,080
Okay.

161
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:06,440
So let me put some other number here like seven.

162
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:08,350
Okay.

163
00:10:08,350 --> 00:10:08,920
Okay.

164
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:15,120
Now I want to operate on that with my elementary matrix.

165
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:17,620
So what's the elementary matrix?

166
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:20,620
Strictly speaking, it's E21, because it's

167
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:26,110
the guy that's going to produce a zero in that position.

168
00:10:26,110 --> 00:10:31,360
And it's going to produce U in one shot,

169
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:35,120
because it's just a two by two matrix.

170
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:40,100
So two one and I'm going to take four of those away from those,

171
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:42,980
produce that zero and leave a three there.

172
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:44,080
And that's U.

173
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:46,920
And what's the matrix that did it?

174
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:48,600
Quick review, then.

175
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:52,680
What's the elimination elementary matrix E21 --

176
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:54,450
it's one zero, thanks.

177
00:10:54,450 --> 00:10:57,530
And -- negative four one,

178
00:10:57,530 --> 00:10:58,120
right.

179
00:10:58,120 --> 00:10:59,520
Good.

180
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:00,360
Okay.

181
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:03,700
So that -- you see the difference between this

182
00:11:03,700 --> 00:11:05,910
and what I'm shooting for.

183
00:11:05,910 --> 00:11:10,370
I'm shooting for A on one side and the other matrices

184
00:11:10,370 --> 00:11:13,101
on the other side of the equation.

185
00:11:13,101 --> 00:11:13,600
Okay.

186
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:17,810
So I can do that right away.

187
00:11:17,810 --> 00:11:21,390
Now here's going to be my A equals L U.

188
00:11:21,390 --> 00:11:24,420
And you won't have any trouble telling me what --

189
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:27,350
so A is still two one eight seven.

190
00:11:30,040 --> 00:11:35,000
L is what you're going to tell me and U is still two one

191
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,280
zero three.

192
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:37,100
Okay.

193
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:42,030
So what's L in this case?

194
00:11:42,030 --> 00:11:46,960
Well, first -- so how is L related to this E guy?

195
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:52,630
It's the inverse, because I want to multiply through

196
00:11:52,630 --> 00:11:56,370
by the inverse of this, which will put the identity here,

197
00:11:56,370 --> 00:12:00,560
and the inverse will show up there and I'll call it L.

198
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:04,450
So what is the inverse of this?

199
00:12:04,450 --> 00:12:09,540
Remember those elimination matrices are easy to invert.

200
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:21,740
The inverse matrix for this one is 1 0 4 1, it's actually flip

201
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:24,210
STRANG: Zero one zero. sign.

202
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:24,780
Okay.

203
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:26,510
Do you want --

204
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:29,960
if we did the numbers right, we must --

205
00:12:29,960 --> 00:12:31,980
this should be correct.

206
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:32,550
Okay.

207
00:12:32,550 --> 00:12:33,940
And of course it is.

208
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:37,820
That says the first row's right, four times the first row

209
00:12:37,820 --> 00:12:41,130
plus the second row is eight seven.

210
00:12:41,130 --> 00:12:41,630
Good.

211
00:12:41,630 --> 00:12:42,800
That's simple, two by two.

212
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:43,300
Okay.

213
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:49,360
But it already shows the form that we're headed for.

214
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:53,360
It shows -- so what's the L stand for?

215
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:54,540
Why the letter L?

216
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:58,880
If U stood for upper triangular, then of course L

217
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:01,960
stands for lower triangular.

218
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:05,955
And actually, it has ones on the diagonal, where this thing has

219
00:13:05,955 --> 00:13:07,080
the pivots on the diagonal.

220
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:16,250
Oh, sometimes we may want to separate out the pivots,

221
00:13:16,250 --> 00:13:20,490
so can I just mention that sometimes we could also write

222
00:13:20,490 --> 00:13:22,030
this as --

223
00:13:22,030 --> 00:13:25,870
we could have this one zero four one --

224
00:13:25,870 --> 00:13:28,760
I'll just show you how I would divide out this matrix

225
00:13:28,760 --> 00:13:31,210
of pivots -- two three.

226
00:13:31,210 --> 00:13:34,020
There's a diagonal matrix.

227
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:38,220
And I just -- whatever is left is here.

228
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:41,670
Now what's left?

229
00:13:41,670 --> 00:13:46,130
If I divide this first row by two to pull out the two,

230
00:13:46,130 --> 00:13:49,280
then I have a one and a one half.

231
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:52,670
And if I divide the second row by three to pull out the three,

232
00:13:52,670 --> 00:13:53,420
then I have a one.

233
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:58,750
Where shall we put them?4 So if this is L U,

234
00:13:58,750 --> 00:14:03,500
this is maybe called L D or pivot U.

235
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:06,710
And now it's a little more balanced,

236
00:14:06,710 --> 00:14:11,770
because we have ones on the diagonal here and here.

237
00:14:11,770 --> 00:14:16,480
And the diagonal matrix in the middle.

238
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:19,180
So both of those...

239
00:14:19,180 --> 00:14:22,550
Matlab would produce either one.

240
00:14:22,550 --> 00:14:26,590
I'll basically stay with L U.

241
00:14:26,590 --> 00:14:27,270
Okay.

242
00:14:27,270 --> 00:14:32,080
Now I have to think about bigger than two by two.

243
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:36,150
But right now, this was just like easy exercise.

244
00:14:36,150 --> 00:14:41,330
And, to tell the truth, this one was a minus sign

245
00:14:41,330 --> 00:14:43,840
and this one was a plus sign.

246
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:47,020
I mean, that's the only difference.

247
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:52,220
But, with three by three, there's

248
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:55,090
a more significant difference.

249
00:14:55,090 --> 00:14:58,030
Let me show you how that works.

250
00:14:58,030 --> 00:15:02,730
Let me move up to a three by three,

251
00:15:02,730 --> 00:15:06,885
let's say some matrix A, okay?

252
00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:12,550
Let's imagine it's three by three.

253
00:15:12,550 --> 00:15:15,450
I won't write numbers down for now.

254
00:15:15,450 --> 00:15:17,890
So what's the first elimination step

255
00:15:17,890 --> 00:15:21,120
that I do, the first matrix I multiply it by,

256
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:23,020
what letter will I use for

257
00:15:23,020 --> 00:15:23,520
that?

258
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:26,090
It'll be E two one, because it's --

259
00:15:26,090 --> 00:15:30,410
the first step will be to get a zero in that two one position.

260
00:15:30,410 --> 00:15:30,910
right?

261
00:15:30,910 --> 00:15:35,400
Okay, now a second row -- say zero zero one and the third guy

262
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:39,210
And then the next step will be to get a zero in the three one

263
00:15:39,210 --> 00:15:40,380
position.

264
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:45,830
And the final step will be to get a zero in the three two

265
00:15:45,830 --> 00:15:51,310
That's what elimination is, and it produced U. position.

266
00:15:51,310 --> 00:15:59,360
