529
00:32:35,659 --> 00:32:36,700
How many have I got here?

530
00:32:36,700 --> 00:32:36,760
Okay.

531
00:32:36,760 --> 00:32:37,801
So, I think -- let's see.

532
00:32:44,450 --> 00:32:51,050
It's about -- well, how many, roughly?

533
00:32:51,050 --> 00:32:54,690
How many operations to get from here to here?

534
00:32:54,690 --> 00:32:57,170
Well, maybe one way to look at it

535
00:32:57,170 --> 00:33:00,670
is all these numbers had to get changed.

536
00:33:00,670 --> 00:33:03,000
The first row didn't get changed,

537
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:06,780
but all the other rows got changed at this step.

538
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:10,770
So this step -- well, I guess maybe --

539
00:33:10,770 --> 00:33:17,135
shall I say it cost about a hundred squared.

540
00:33:20,710 --> 00:33:23,820
I mean, if I had changed the first row,

541
00:33:23,820 --> 00:33:27,340
then it would have been exactly hundred squared, because --

542
00:33:27,340 --> 00:33:30,600
because that's how many numbers are here.

543
00:33:30,600 --> 00:33:34,680
A hundred squared numbers is the total count of the entry,

544
00:33:34,680 --> 00:33:39,170
and all but this insignificant first row got changed.

545
00:33:39,170 --> 00:33:42,540
So I would say about a hundred squared.

546
00:33:42,540 --> 00:33:43,450
Okay.

547
00:33:43,450 --> 00:33:45,100
Now, what about the next step?

548
00:33:49,060 --> 00:33:51,860
So now the first row is fine.

549
00:33:51,860 --> 00:33:54,920
The second row is fine.

550
00:33:54,920 --> 00:33:58,880
And I'm changing these zeroes are all fine,

551
00:33:58,880 --> 00:34:01,370
so what's up with the second step?

552
00:34:01,370 --> 00:34:03,430
And then you're with me.

553
00:34:03,430 --> 00:34:05,220
Roughly, what's the cost?

554
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:08,610
If this first step cost a hundred squared,

555
00:34:08,610 --> 00:34:12,150
about, operations then this one, which

556
00:34:12,150 --> 00:34:18,912
is really working on this guy to produce this, costs about what?

557
00:34:22,449 --> 00:34:26,000
How many operations to fix?

558
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:30,620
About ninety-nine squared, or ninety-nine times ninety-eight.

559
00:34:30,620 --> 00:34:31,780
But less, right?

560
00:34:31,780 --> 00:34:34,320
Less, because our problem's getting smaller.

561
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:37,164
About ninety-nine squared.

562
00:34:37,164 --> 00:34:39,330
And then I go down and down and the next one will be

563
00:34:39,330 --> 00:34:41,690
ninety-eight squared, the next ninety-seven squared

564
00:34:41,690 --> 00:34:44,650
and finally I'm down around one squared or --

565
00:34:44,650 --> 00:34:48,830
where it's just like the little numbers.

566
00:34:48,830 --> 00:34:51,280
The big numbers are here.

567
00:34:51,280 --> 00:34:58,860
So the number of operations is about n squared plus that

568
00:34:58,860 --> 00:35:01,560
was n, right? n was a hundred?

569
00:35:01,560 --> 00:35:06,260
n squared for the first step, then n minus one squared,

570
00:35:06,260 --> 00:35:10,470
then n minus two squared, finally down to three squared

571
00:35:10,470 --> 00:35:13,134
and two squared and even one squared.

572
00:35:18,160 --> 00:35:22,280
No way I should have written that --

573
00:35:22,280 --> 00:35:23,110
squeezed that in.

574
00:35:23,110 --> 00:35:31,070
Let me try it so the count is N squared plus N minus one

575
00:35:31,070 --> 00:35:33,385
squared plus -- all the way down to one squared.

576
00:35:36,890 --> 00:35:39,950
That's a pretty decent count.

577
00:35:39,950 --> 00:35:45,520
Admittedly, we didn't catch every single tiny operation,

578
00:35:45,520 --> 00:35:52,230
but we got the right leading term here.

579
00:35:52,230 --> 00:35:56,700
And what do those add up to?

580
00:35:56,700 --> 00:36:02,840
Okay, so now we're coming to the punch of this,

581
00:36:02,840 --> 00:36:05,500
question, this operation count.

582
00:36:05,500 --> 00:36:09,980
So the operations on the left side, on the matrix A

583
00:36:09,980 --> 00:36:15,020
to finally get to U.

584
00:36:15,020 --> 00:36:20,500
And anybody -- so which of these quantities is the right

585
00:36:20,500 --> 00:36:24,290
ballpark for that count?

586
00:36:24,290 --> 00:36:27,447
If I add a hundred squared to ninety nine squared to ninety

587
00:36:27,447 --> 00:36:29,030
eight squared -- ninety seven squared,

588
00:36:29,030 --> 00:36:34,020
all the way down to two squared then one squared,

589
00:36:34,020 --> 00:36:36,760
what have I got, about?

590
00:36:36,760 --> 00:36:43,390
It's just one of these -- let's identify it first.

591
00:36:43,390 --> 00:36:44,290
Is it N?

592
00:36:44,290 --> 00:36:47,220
Certainly not.

593
00:36:47,220 --> 00:36:50,980
Is it n factorial?

594
00:36:50,980 --> 00:36:51,520
No.

595
00:36:51,520 --> 00:36:54,740
If it was n factorial, we would --

596
00:36:54,740 --> 00:36:57,380
with determinants, it is n factorial.

597
00:36:57,380 --> 00:37:04,490
I'll put in a bad mark against determinants, because that --

598
00:37:04,490 --> 00:37:08,750
okay, so what is it?

599
00:37:08,750 --> 00:37:12,740
It's n -- well, this is the answer.

600
00:37:12,740 --> 00:37:16,610
It's this order -- n cubed.

601
00:37:16,610 --> 00:37:21,920
It's like I have n terms, right?

602
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:24,290
I've got n terms in this sum.

603
00:37:24,290 --> 00:37:26,400
And the biggest one is N squared.

604
00:37:26,400 --> 00:37:29,420
So the worst it could be would be n cubed,

605
00:37:29,420 --> 00:37:32,880
but it's not as bad as --

606
00:37:32,880 --> 00:37:35,670
it's N cubed times --

607
00:37:35,670 --> 00:37:40,780
it's about one third of n cubed.

608
00:37:40,780 --> 00:37:49,240
That's the magic operation count.

609
00:37:49,240 --> 00:37:52,370
Somehow that one third takes account of the fact

610
00:37:52,370 --> 00:37:57,740
that the numbers are getting smaller.

611
00:37:57,740 --> 00:37:59,280
If they weren't getting smaller, we

612
00:37:59,280 --> 00:38:01,430
would have n terms times n squared,

613
00:38:01,430 --> 00:38:03,180
but it would be exactly n cubed.

614
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:06,180
But our numbers are getting smaller -- actually,

615
00:38:06,180 --> 00:38:09,630
row two and row one moves down to row three.

616
00:38:09,630 --> 00:38:13,430
do you remember where does one third come in this --

617
00:38:13,430 --> 00:38:15,840
I'll even allow a mention of calculus.

618
00:38:15,840 --> 00:38:17,910
So calculus can be mentioned, integration

619
00:38:17,910 --> 00:38:23,630
can be mentioned now in the next minute and not again for weeks.

620
00:38:23,630 --> 00:38:28,970
It's not that I don't like 18.01, but18.06 is better.

621
00:38:28,970 --> 00:38:29,780
Okay.

622
00:38:29,780 --> 00:38:38,240
So, -- so what's -- what's the calculus formula that looks

623
00:38:38,240 --> 00:38:38,830
like?

624
00:38:38,830 --> 00:38:42,530
It looks like -- if we were in calculus instead of summing

625
00:38:42,530 --> 00:38:46,090
stuff, we would integrate.

626
00:38:46,090 --> 00:38:52,460
So I would integrate x squared and I would get one third x

627
00:38:52,460 --> 00:38:53,400
cubed.

628
00:38:53,400 --> 00:39:00,720
So if that was like an integral from one to N,

629
00:39:00,720 --> 00:39:05,520
of x squared b x, if the answer would be one third n cubed --

630
00:39:05,520 --> 00:39:09,240
and it's correct for the sum also, because that's, like,

631
00:39:09,240 --> 00:39:10,410
the whole point of calculus.

632
00:39:10,410 --> 00:39:12,410
The whole point of calculus is -- oh,

633
00:39:12,410 --> 00:39:13,951
I don't want to tell you the whole --

634
00:39:13,951 --> 00:39:18,970
I mean, you know the whole point of calculus.

635
00:39:18,970 --> 00:39:24,170
Calculus is like sums except it's continuous.

636
00:39:24,170 --> 00:39:24,910
Okay.

637
00:39:24,910 --> 00:39:27,880
And algebra is discreet.

638
00:39:27,880 --> 00:39:28,560
Okay.

639
00:39:28,560 --> 00:39:30,740
So the answer is one third n cubed.

640
00:39:30,740 --> 00:39:35,680
Now I'll just -- let me say one more thing about operations.

641
00:39:35,680 --> 00:39:37,510
What about the right-hand side?

642
00:39:37,510 --> 00:39:40,840
This was what it cost on the left side.

643
00:39:40,840 --> 00:39:44,880
This is on A.

644
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:47,930
Because this is A that we're working with.

645
00:39:47,930 --> 00:39:52,800
But what's the cost on the extra column vector b

646
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:54,790
that we're hanging around here?

647
00:39:54,790 --> 00:40:00,270
So b costs a lot less, obviously,

648
00:40:00,270 --> 00:40:03,270
because it's just one column.

649
00:40:03,270 --> 00:40:07,760
We carry it through elimination and then

650
00:40:07,760 --> 00:40:10,020
actually we do back substitution.

651
00:40:10,020 --> 00:40:11,940
Let me just tell you the answer there.

652
00:40:11,940 --> 00:40:14,200
It's n squared.

653
00:40:14,200 --> 00:40:18,951
So the cost for every right hand side is n squared.

654
00:40:18,951 --> 00:40:19,450
So let me --

655
00:40:19,450 --> 00:40:30,250
I'll just fit that in here -- for the the cost of b turns out

656
00:40:30,250 --> 00:40:32,790
to be n squared.

657
00:40:32,790 --> 00:40:37,920
So you see if we have, as we often

658
00:40:37,920 --> 00:40:43,010
have, a a matrix A and several right-hand sides,

659
00:40:43,010 --> 00:40:50,520
then we pay the price on A, the higher price on A to get it

660
00:40:50,520 --> 00:40:54,470
split up into L and U to do elimination on A,

661
00:40:54,470 --> 00:40:58,930
but then we can process every right-hand side at low cost.

662
00:40:58,930 --> 00:40:59,700
Okay.

663
00:40:59,700 --> 00:41:05,730
So the -- We really have discussed the most fundamental

664
00:41:05,730 --> 00:41:12,441
algorithm for a system of equations.

665
00:41:12,441 --> 00:41:12,940
Okay.

666
00:41:15,590 --> 00:41:24,210
So, I'm ready to allow row exchanges.

667
00:41:24,210 --> 00:41:28,430
I'm ready to allow -- now what happens to this whole --

668
00:41:28,430 --> 00:41:30,830
today's lecture if there are row exchanges?

669
00:41:34,170 --> 00:41:38,090
When would there be row exchanges?

670
00:41:38,090 --> 00:41:41,430
There are row -- we need to do row exchanges if a zero shows

671
00:41:41,430 --> 00:41:42,610
up in the pivot position.

672
00:41:42,610 --> 00:41:48,240
So moving then into the final section of this chapter,

673
00:41:48,240 --> 00:41:51,810
which is about transposes -- well,

674
00:41:51,810 --> 00:41:56,640
we've already seen some transposes, and --

675
00:41:56,640 --> 00:42:01,060
the title of this section is,

676
00:42:01,060 --> 00:42:04,480
So tell -- there is another one here.

677
00:42:04,480 --> 00:42:09,450
"Transposes and Permutations."

678
00:42:09,450 --> 00:42:11,320
Okay.

679
00:42:11,320 --> 00:42:16,740
So can I say, now, where does a permutation come in?

680
00:42:16,740 --> 00:42:19,346
Let me talk a little about permutations.

681
00:42:19,346 --> 00:42:20,845
So that'll be up here, permutations.

682
00:42:27,530 --> 00:42:35,890
So these are the matrices that I need to do row exchanges.

683
00:42:35,890 --> 00:42:40,570
And I may have to do two row exchanges.

684
00:42:40,570 --> 00:42:42,580
Can you invent a matrix where I would

685
00:42:42,580 --> 00:42:50,280
have to do two row exchanges and then would come out fine?

686
00:42:50,280 --> 00:42:53,640
Yeah let's just, for the heck of it --

687
00:42:53,640 --> 00:42:57,580
so I'll put it here.

688
00:42:57,580 --> 00:43:00,240
Let me do three by threes.

689
00:43:00,240 --> 00:43:03,840
Actually, why don't I just plain list

690
00:43:03,840 --> 00:43:06,610
all the three by three permutation matrices.

691
00:43:06,610 --> 00:43:09,030
There're a nice little group of them.

692
00:43:09,030 --> 00:43:16,757
What are all the matrices that exchange no rows at all?

693
00:43:16,757 --> 00:43:18,090
Well, I'll include the identity.

694
00:43:22,270 --> 00:43:25,870
So that's a permutation matrix that doesn't do anything.

695
00:43:25,870 --> 00:43:30,010
Now what's the permutation matrix that exchanges --

696
00:43:30,010 --> 00:43:31,510
what is P12?

697
00:43:31,510 --> 00:43:36,980
The permutation matrix that exchanges rows one and two

698
00:43:36,980 --> 00:43:39,280
would be --

699
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:42,940
0 1 0 --

700
00:43:42,940 --> 00:43:44,770
1 0 0, right.

701
00:43:44,770 --> 00:43:49,670
I just exchanged those rows of the identity and I've got it.

702
00:43:49,670 --> 00:43:50,210
Okay.

703
00:43:50,210 --> 00:43:51,970
Actually, I'll -- yes.

704
00:43:51,970 --> 00:43:54,670
Let me clutter this up.

705
00:43:54,670 --> 00:43:55,170
Okay.

706
00:43:58,900 --> 00:44:02,610
Give me a complete list of all the row exchange matrices.

707
00:44:02,610 --> 00:44:03,720
So what are they?

708
00:44:03,720 --> 00:44:07,490
They're all the ways I can take the identity matrix

709
00:44:07,490 --> 00:44:10,050
and rearrange its rows.

710
00:44:10,050 --> 00:44:13,320
How many will there be?

711
00:44:13,320 --> 00:44:18,160
How many three by three permutation matrices?

712
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:20,430
Shall we keep going and get the answer?

713
00:44:20,430 --> 00:44:21,740
So tell me some more.

714
00:44:21,740 --> 00:44:28,512
What one are you going to do now?

715
00:44:28,512 --> 00:44:29,970
I'm going to switch rows one and --

716
00:44:29,970 --> 00:44:30,170
One and three, okay.

717
00:44:30,170 --> 00:44:30,380
One and three, leaving two alone.

718
00:44:30,380 --> 00:44:30,420
Now what else?

719
00:44:30,420 --> 00:44:30,440
Okay.

720
00:44:30,440 --> 00:44:30,540
Switch -- what would be the next easy one --

721
00:44:30,540 --> 00:44:31,790
is switch two and three, good.

722
00:44:31,790 --> 00:44:50,120
So I'll leave one zero zero alone and I'll switch --

723
00:44:50,120 --> 00:44:56,770
I'll move number three up and number two down.

724
00:44:56,770 --> 00:44:57,700
Okay.

725
00:44:57,700 --> 00:45:01,690
Those are the ones that just exchange single -- a pair of

726
00:45:01,690 --> 00:45:02,510
rows.

727
00:45:02,510 --> 00:45:06,580
This guy, this guy and this guy exchanges a pair of rows,

728
00:45:06,580 --> 00:45:08,720
but now there are more possibilities.

729
00:45:08,720 --> 00:45:10,740
What's left?

730
00:45:10,740 --> 00:45:17,810
And there's one more, which is --

731
00:45:17,810 --> 00:45:19,830
let's see.

732
00:45:19,830 --> 00:45:21,850
What's left?

733
00:45:21,850 --> 00:45:23,870
I'm lost.

734
00:45:23,870 --> 00:45:28,920
STUDENT: Is it zero zero one?

735
00:45:28,920 --> 00:45:29,940
STRANG: Okay.

736
00:45:29,940 --> 00:45:32,980
STUDENT: One zero zero.

737
00:45:32,980 --> 00:45:37,030
STRANG: One zero zero, okay.

738
00:45:37,030 --> 00:45:40,060
Zero one zero.

739
00:45:40,060 --> 00:45:41,070
Okay.

740
00:45:41,070 --> 00:45:42,080
Great.

741
00:45:42,080 --> 00:45:43,090
Six.

742
00:45:43,090 --> 00:45:46,120
Six of them.

743
00:45:46,120 --> 00:45:55,210
Six P. And they're sort of nice, because what

744
00:45:55,210 --> 00:46:04,290
happens if I write, multiply two of them together?

745
00:46:04,290 --> 00:46:12,370
If I multiply two of these matrices together,

746
00:46:12,370 --> 00:46:20,450
what can you tell me about the answer?

747
00:46:20,450 --> 00:46:24,490
It's on the list.

748
00:46:24,490 --> 00:46:33,570
If I do some row exchanges and then I

749
00:46:33,570 --> 00:46:41,650
do some more row exchanges, then all together

750
00:46:41,650 --> 00:46:45,690
I've done row exchanges.

751
00:46:45,690 --> 00:46:51,130
So if I multiply -- but, I don't know.

752
00:46:51,130 --> 00:46:55,950
And if I invert, then I'm just doing row exchanges

753
00:46:55,950 --> 00:46:57,020
to get back again.

754
00:46:57,020 --> 00:46:58,850
So the inverses are all there.

755
00:46:58,850 --> 00:47:03,350
It's a little family of matrices that --

756
00:47:03,350 --> 00:47:08,780
they've got their own -- if I multiply,

757
00:47:08,780 --> 00:47:11,000
I'm still inside this group.

758
00:47:11,000 --> 00:47:13,280
If I invert I'm inside this group -- actually,

759
00:47:13,280 --> 00:47:15,600
group is the right name for this subject.

760
00:47:15,600 --> 00:47:21,020
It's a group of six matrices, and what about the inverses?

761
00:47:21,020 --> 00:47:24,030
What's the inverse of this guy, for example?

762
00:47:24,030 --> 00:47:27,740
What's the inverse -- if I exchange rows one and two,

763
00:47:27,740 --> 00:47:31,330
what's the inverse matrix?

764
00:47:31,330 --> 00:47:33,260
Just tell me fast.

765
00:47:33,260 --> 00:47:37,540
The inverse of that matrix is --

766
00:47:37,540 --> 00:47:42,240
if I exchange rows one and two, then

767
00:47:42,240 --> 00:47:46,300
what I should do to get back to where I started

768
00:47:46,300 --> 00:47:47,920
is the same thing.

769
00:47:47,920 --> 00:47:51,700
So this thing is its own inverse.

770
00:47:51,700 --> 00:47:53,200
That's probably its own inverse.

771
00:47:53,200 --> 00:47:54,740
This is probably not -- actually,

772
00:47:54,740 --> 00:47:57,150
I think these are inverses of each other.

773
00:47:57,150 --> 00:48:00,200
Oh, yeah, actually --

774
00:48:00,200 --> 00:48:02,890
the inverse is the transpose.

775
00:48:02,890 --> 00:48:08,780
There's a curious fact about permutations matrices,

776
00:48:08,780 --> 00:48:12,780
that the inverses are the transposes.

777
00:48:12,780 --> 00:48:16,060
And final moment -- how many are there if I --

778
00:48:16,060 --> 00:48:18,640
how many four by four permutations?

779
00:48:18,640 --> 00:48:22,070
So let me take four by four --

780
00:48:22,070 --> 00:48:26,740
how many Ps?

781
00:48:26,740 --> 00:48:29,500
Well, okay.

782
00:48:29,500 --> 00:48:31,019
Make a good guess.

783
00:48:31,019 --> 00:48:31,810
Twenty four, right.

784
00:48:31,810 --> 00:48:38,947
Twenty four Ps.

785
00:48:44,788 --> 00:48:45,288
Okay.

786
00:48:51,150 --> 00:49:29,230
So, we've got these permutation matrices,

787
00:49:29,230 --> 00:50:15,200
and in the next lecture, we'll use them.
